(window.PSAC_DEMO_DECKS = window.PSAC_DEMO_DECKS || {})[8] = {"grade":8,"packs":{"grade8-english":{"g8eng-listening":[{"id":"g8eng-listening-015","q":"A speaker describes how her school built a vegetable garden, and mentions in passing that it rained on the opening day.<br>Which detail should a <b>summary</b> leave out?","opts":["That the garden grows vegetables","That the school built a garden","That the garden was opened","That it rained on opening day"],"a":"That it rained on opening day","hint":"Which detail changes nothing if you remove it?","exp":"The weather is an aside: drop it and the account still makes sense. The other three carry the main idea — who did it, what was grown and that it opened — so losing any of them would leave the summary incomplete."},{"id":"g8eng-listening-002","q":"A news reader says: \"The government claims this is the best economic plan in decades.\" What word signals that this might be an OPINION?","opts":["claims","economic","plan","government"],"a":"claims","hint":"When a speaker uses \"claims\" or \"says\", they are reporting another person's position, not a proven fact.","exp":"\"<b>Claims</b>\" signals that the statement is the government's opinion, not an independently verified fact. Signal words for opinion: claims, believes, argues, thinks, according to, in my view. Recognising these signals prevents you from treating opinions as facts."},{"id":"g8eng-listening-017","q":"A pupil is asked to summarise a five-minute talk and writes twelve sentences, repeating each of the speaker's examples in turn.<br>What is wrong with this?","opts":["It uses the wrong tense throughout","It contains far too many opinions","It leaves out the speaker's name","It retells rather than condenses"],"a":"It retells rather than condenses","hint":"How long should a summary be next to the original?","exp":"A summary must be shorter than what it summarises, so keeping every example makes it a retelling. No tense error is described, a summary need not name the speaker, and repeating someone faithfully adds no opinion."},{"id":"g8eng-listening-013","q":"A councillor says: <i>\"The new bus route will cut the journey from Rose Hill to Curepipe by half.\"</i><br>What kind of statement is this?","opts":["A fact recorded in a timetable","A prediction, not yet checkable","A summary of the whole debate","An instruction given to drivers"],"a":"A prediction, not yet checkable","hint":"Has the thing being described already happened?","exp":"The route has not run yet, so there is nothing to measure it against: a claim about the future is a prediction. No timetable records it, it tells nobody to do anything, and it condenses no earlier talk."},{"id":"g8eng-listening-018","q":"Speaker A: <i>\"Free tablets would help every pupil study at home.\"</i><br>Speaker B: <i>\"Tablets help only if the family has data.\"</i><br>What is the <b>point of disagreement</b>?","opts":["Whether every school has a library","Whether tablets cost too much money","Whether pupils should study home","Whether tablets alone are enough"],"a":"Whether tablets alone are enough","hint":"B does not say tablets are useless — read carefully what B adds.","exp":"B accepts that tablets can help but makes that help conditional on data, so the dispute is about sufficiency, not usefulness. Cost and libraries are never mentioned, and both speakers take home study for granted."}],"g8eng-speaking":[{"id":"g8eng-speaking-011","q":"In a class debate two speakers begin talking at the same moment.<br>What should the chairperson do?","opts":["Ask both of them to sit down","Let one finish, then call the other","Repeat the motion from the start","Give the floor to a third speaker"],"a":"Let one finish, then call the other","hint":"A chairperson manages turns; nobody loses their turn for speaking too eagerly.","exp":"The chair's job is to order the turns so both contributions are heard. Silencing both punishes eagerness, restarting the motion wastes the floor, and skipping to a third speaker loses two arguments."},{"id":"g8eng-speaking-002","q":"You want to support your opinion in a discussion. Which phrase introduces evidence MOST effectively?","opts":["\"I just think so.\"","\"According to research, …\"","\"My friend says …\"","\"Obviously everyone knows …\""],"a":"\"According to research, …\"","hint":"Evidence from named, credible sources is more persuasive than personal feelings.","exp":"\"<b>According to research</b>\" introduces credible evidence, which strengthens an argument. \"I just think so\" is an unsupported opinion. \"My friend says\" is anecdote. \"Obviously everyone knows\" is an appeal that assumes agreement without proof."},{"id":"g8eng-speaking-005","q":"At the end of a formal debate, the speaker summarises the argument. Which word signals a CONCLUSION?","opts":["However","For example","Therefore","Furthermore"],"a":"Therefore","hint":"\"Therefore\" signals a conclusion drawn from previous reasoning.","exp":"\"<b>Therefore</b>\" signals a conclusion — \"We have shown X and Y; therefore, we should Z.\" \"Furthermore\" adds more points; \"however\" introduces contrast; \"for example\" introduces an illustration."},{"id":"g8eng-speaking-014","q":"A Grade 8 class must warn Grade 1 pupils about a wet corridor.<br>Which spoken warning suits that audience <b>best</b>?","opts":["The floor is wet — walk slowly","Kindly exercise caution when moving","Be advised the corridor is slippery","Wet surface: proceed with due care"],"a":"The floor is wet — walk slowly","hint":"Which one could a six-year-old act on immediately?","exp":"Short everyday words and a clear instruction reach a Grade 1 listener. \"Exercise caution\", \"proceed with due care\" and \"be advised\" are official phrasings a young child would have to decode before acting on them."},{"id":"g8eng-speaking-017","q":"A speaker claims: <i>\"Every pupil in my class walks to school, so walking must be easy for everyone in Mauritius.\"</i><br>What is the weakness of this argument?","opts":["The sentence is far too long here","One class cannot stand for all","Walking is not defined in the talk","The speaker does not name a source"],"a":"One class cannot stand for all","hint":"How big is the group the speaker has actually observed?","exp":"The speaker generalises from a single class to a whole country, which the sample is far too small to support. The class is a source, the meaning of walking is plain, and length is not what makes the reasoning fail."}],"g8eng-reading":[{"id":"g8eng-reading-013","q":"Read: <i>\"Sanjay checked the fridge, then the cupboard, then the fridge again.\"</i><br>What does this <b>imply</b>?","opts":["He has lost something small","He is tidying the kitchen","There is nothing to eat","He is cooking a large meal"],"a":"There is nothing to eat","hint":"Why would anyone look in the same place twice?","exp":"Returning to a place already searched shows the search has failed, and a fridge is searched for food. Tidying, hunting for an object and cooking would all involve doing something, not just looking again."},{"id":"g8eng-reading-008","q":"Read: \"The test results sat unopened on the desk for three days.\" What can you INFER about the character?","opts":["They had already passed the test.","They had forgotten about the test.","They were nervous or afraid of the results.","They were too busy to open the envelope."],"a":"They were nervous or afraid of the results.","hint":"Why would someone leave important news unopened for three days?","exp":"Leaving results unopened for three days suggests <b>fear or anxiety</b> about the outcome. This is implicit — never stated directly. The reader infers the character's emotional state from the action described."},{"id":"g8eng-reading-016","q":"A school notice reads: <i>\"Pupils are reminded that the gates close at 07:45. No exceptions will be made.\"</i><br>What is the <b>tone</b>?","opts":["Firm and official","Playful and teasing","Warm and personal","Anxious and unsure"],"a":"Firm and official","hint":"Read the last four words again and ask how much room they leave.","exp":"The passive \"are reminded\" and the flat refusal of exceptions leave no room to negotiate, which is a firm official tone. Nothing here is affectionate, joking or hesitant."},{"id":"g8eng-reading-015","q":"Read: <i>\"Mum read the electricity bill twice, folded it, and said nothing about the new fan.\"</i><br>What can you infer?","opts":["She cannot read the bill","She has bought the fan","The fan is already broken","The bill worries her"],"a":"The bill worries her","hint":"What is the link between reading a bill twice and staying silent about a purchase?","exp":"Rereading and then avoiding the subject of the fan suggests the amount troubles her. She clearly can read it, the fan is described as new rather than bought by her, and nothing hints that it has failed."},{"id":"g8eng-reading-019","q":"Read: <i>\"Every year the shop repainted its sign. This year the paint tins stayed in the yard, unopened.\"</i><br>What does the second sentence <b>imply</b>?","opts":["The sign was painted much earlier","The owner has bought new tins","The shop has changed its colours","Something has changed at the shop"],"a":"Something has changed at the shop","hint":"The writer sets up a habit, then breaks it. Why?","exp":"A yearly routine that stops for the first time signals a change the writer has not yet named. No new colour is described, the tins are the usual ones, and the point is that this year they were never used."}]},"grade8-french":{"g8fr-co":[{"id":"g8fr-co-004","q":"Dans un schéma de communication, le \"code\" désigne :","opts":["Le secret du message","La qualité du son","La durée du message","La langue employée"],"a":"La langue employée","hint":"Pour se comprendre, émetteur et récepteur doivent partager le même code.","exp":"Le <b>code</b> est le système de signes partagé entre l'émetteur et le récepteur — généralement la langue parlée ou écrite, mais aussi les gestes, les symboles."},{"id":"g8fr-co-011","q":"Un reportage radio sur un événement est un texte oral de type :","opts":["Informatif et descriptif","Argumentatif seulement","Injonctif","Poétique"],"a":"Informatif et descriptif","hint":"Un reportage informe sur des faits et décrit la situation.","exp":"Un <b>reportage</b> combine le type <b>informatif</b> (il rapporte des faits) et <b>descriptif</b> (il décrit l'ambiance, les personnes, les lieux)."},{"id":"g8fr-co-012","q":"Différencier le sens littéral et le sens figuré d'une expression signifie :","opts":["Lire entre les lignes uniquement","Compter les syllabes des mots","Distinguer le propre et le figuré","Trouver la rime de la phrase"],"a":"Distinguer le propre et le figuré","hint":"\"Avoir le cafard\" au sens figuré signifie être triste — pas qu'un insecte est là.","exp":"Le <b>sens littéral</b> (propre) = le sens direct des mots. Le <b>sens figuré</b> = le sens imagé, métaphorique. Ex. : \"Casser les pieds\" (sens figuré = ennuyer, pas casser vraiment)."},{"id":"g8fr-co-008","q":"Quand un locuteur dit \"Bien sûr, ça se voit !\" avec un ton moqueur, il exprime :","opts":["De l'admiration","De la tristesse","De l'ironie","De la surprise"],"a":"De l'ironie","hint":"L'ironie, c'est quand le ton contredit les mots.","exp":"L'<b>ironie</b> consiste à dire le contraire de ce qu'on pense, souvent avec un ton moqueur. Le ton est crucial pour distinguer l'ironie de l'affirmation sincère."},{"id":"g8fr-hd-007","q":"<i>\"Apr&egrave;s le passage du cyclone, la route &eacute;tait <b>coup&eacute;e</b> : personne ne pouvait rejoindre le village avant lundi.\"</i> Que signifie &laquo; coup&eacute;e &raquo; dans cette phrase ?","opts":["Bloquée, impossible à emprunter","Repeinte récemment par la commune","Interrompue par un feu rouge","Tranchée en deux morceaux avec une lame"],"a":"Bloquée, impossible à emprunter","hint":"Sers-toi de la suite : <i>personne ne pouvait rejoindre le village</i>.","exp":"Le sens courant de &laquo; couper &raquo; (trancher avec une lame) est le pi&egrave;ge : ici c'est le <b>contexte</b> qui d&eacute;cide. Puisque plus personne ne passe pendant plusieurs jours, &laquo; coup&eacute;e &raquo; veut dire que la circulation est impossible. Un feu rouge n'arr&ecirc;terait personne jusqu'&agrave; lundi."}],"g8fr-eo":[{"id":"g8fr-eo-011","q":"Dans un groupe de discussion, le modérateur a pour rôle de :","opts":["Écrire les réponses sur le tableau","Parler le plus longtemps possible","Décider qui a raison et qui a tort","Distribuer la parole au groupe"],"a":"Distribuer la parole au groupe","hint":"Le modérateur est comme un arbitre de la conversation.","exp":"Le <b>modérateur</b> (ou animateur) distribue la parole, recentre les débats sur le sujet, s'assure que tout le monde peut s'exprimer et que les échanges restent respectueux."},{"id":"g8fr-eo-002","q":"Les didascalies dans un texte de théâtre sont :","opts":["La morale finale de la pièce","Des indications de mise en scène","Les paroles dites par les personnages","Le titre imprimé de la pièce"],"a":"Des indications de mise en scène","hint":"Ce sont les instructions de l'auteur pour les acteurs et le metteur en scène.","exp":"Les <b>didascalies</b> sont les indications que l'auteur donne pour la mise en scène : comment le personnage doit parler, bouger, quels décors utiliser. Elles sont écrites en italique ou entre parenthèses."},{"id":"g8fr-eo-009","q":"Quel registre de langue utilise-t-on avec un directeur d'école ?","opts":["Le registre familier","Le registre vulgaire","Le registre soutenu","Le registre argotique"],"a":"Le registre soutenu","hint":"On adapte son langage à la personne à qui on s'adresse.","exp":"Le <b>registre soutenu</b> est utilisé dans des situations formelles (avec un directeur, un médecin, lors d'un exposé). Le registre <b>familier</b> est pour les amis et la famille."},{"id":"g8fr-eo-006","q":"Pour exprimer son désaccord poliment à l'oral, on peut dire :","opts":["\"Tu as absolument tort, tais-toi donc !\"","\"Je comprends, mais je ne crois pas cela.\"","\"C'est ridicule, je ne t'écoute plus.\"","\"N'importe quoi, tu racontes des bêtises !\""],"a":"\"Je comprends, mais je ne crois pas cela.\"","hint":"La politesse et le respect sont essentiels dans tout échange oral.","exp":"Exprimer son <b>désaccord poliment</b> implique de reconnaître d'abord la position de l'autre (\"Je comprends...\") avant d'introduire son propre point de vue (\"mais je ne crois pas cela\", \"cependant...\"). Cela maintient un échange respectueux."},{"id":"g8fr-eo-012","q":"Lorsqu'on lit un poème à voix haute, qu'est-ce qu'une \"diérèse\" ?","opts":["Une faute d'orthographe dans le vers","Deux voyelles en deux syllabes","Deux voyelles en une seule syllabe","Une pause marquée à la fin du vers"],"a":"Deux voyelles en deux syllabes","hint":"La diérèse rallonge un mot en séparant deux voyelles.","exp":"La <b>diérèse</b> est la prononciation de deux voyelles voisines en deux syllabes (ex. : \"lion\" = li-on). Son contraire, la <b>synérèse</b>, les fusionne en une seule syllabe."}],"g8fr-ce":[{"id":"g8fr-ce-003","q":"Pour dégager l'idée principale d'un texte complexe, on peut :","opts":["S'arrêter au tout premier paragraphe","Compter le nombre de mots par phrase","Résumer chaque paragraphe, puis relier","Lire uniquement les titres du texte"],"a":"Résumer chaque paragraphe, puis relier","hint":"Chaque paragraphe développe une idée partielle liée au tout.","exp":"Une méthode efficace : lire chaque paragraphe, dégager sa <b>phrase clé</b>, puis chercher l'idée qui les <b>relie toutes</b> — c'est l'idée principale du texte."},{"id":"g8fr-ce-015","q":"Pour repérer les informations implicites dans un texte, on doit :","opts":["Analyser les mots et le contexte","Lire uniquement les mots en gras","Ignorer les adverbes du texte","Compter les paragraphes du texte"],"a":"Analyser les mots et le contexte","hint":"L'implicite se cache dans les détails : les mots choisis, les gestes, le contexte.","exp":"Les informations implicites se trouvent dans les <b>choix lexicaux</b> (mots positifs ou négatifs), les <b>actions des personnages</b> et le <b>contexte</b>. Il faut analyser ces éléments pour déduire ce que l'auteur ne dit pas directement."},{"id":"g8fr-ce-009","q":"Comparer deux textes sur le même sujet consiste à :","opts":["Choisir lequel des deux est le meilleur","Les copier l'un après l'autre","Voir ce qui les rapproche et les sépare","Les lire tous les deux très vite"],"a":"Voir ce qui les rapproche et les sépare","hint":"Comparer = noter ce qui est pareil et ce qui est différent.","exp":"<b>Comparer deux textes</b> implique d'identifier : les ressemblances (même sujet, même type de texte) et les différences (point de vue, registre, arguments, structure)."},{"id":"g8fr-ce-010","q":"Un auteur écrit : \"Les enfants couraient, riaient, criaient dans la cour.\" Quelle information est implicite ?","opts":["Les enfants sont heureux","Il fait nuit dans la cour","La cour est vraiment petite","Les enfants sont en colère"],"a":"Les enfants sont heureux","hint":"Les verbes \"courir, rire, crier\" évoquent une ambiance joyeuse.","exp":"La phrase ne dit pas explicitement que les enfants sont heureux, mais les verbes <b>courir, rire, crier</b> dans la cour l'impliquent fortement. C'est une information <b>implicite</b>."},{"id":"g8fr-ce-012","q":"La personnification est une figure de style qui :","opts":["Compare deux personnes l'une à l'autre","Donne des qualités humaines à un objet","Cache le sens réel du texte écrit","Répète la même idée deux fois"],"a":"Donne des qualités humaines à un objet","hint":"Ex. : \"La mer est en colère\" — la mer ne peut pas ressentir de colère.","exp":"La <b>personnification</b> consiste à attribuer des qualités ou des actions humaines à une chose inanimée, un animal ou une idée abstraite. Ex. : \"Le vent hurle dans la nuit.\""}]},"grade8-maths":{"g8m-real-numbers":[{"id":"g8m-real-012","q":"Which of the following represents a rational number on a number line?","opts":["√5","√2","√3","√9"],"a":"√9","hint":"Which of these square roots gives a whole number?","exp":"<b>√9 = 3</b>, a whole number with an exact position on the number line at 3. The others are irrational and have approximate decimal positions."},{"id":"g8m-real-044","q":"Simplify √3 × √12.","opts":["2√3","√36","6","3√4"],"a":"6","hint":"√3 × √12 = √(3 × 12) = √36.","exp":"√3 × √12 = √36 = <b>6</b>."},{"id":"g8m-hd-006","q":"Three points are marked on a number line: <b>P = 3/4</b>, <b>Q = &radic;2</b> and <b>R = 1.2</b>.<br>Which point lies nearest to 1?","opts":["Q, because &radic;2 is 1.41 from 1","R, because it is 0.2 from 1","P, because it is 0.25 from 1","P, because 3/4 is the smallest value"],"a":"R, because it is 0.2 from 1","hint":"Write all three as decimals, then measure each distance from 1.","exp":"P = 0.75 is 0.25 away, R = 1.2 is 0.2 away and Q = &radic;2 &asymp; 1.41 is about 0.41 away, so <b>R</b> is nearest. Being the smallest value does not make P the nearest, and &radic;2 is 1.41 itself, not 1.41 away from 1."},{"id":"g8m-hd-007","q":"One square field has an area of <b>1 800 m&sup2;</b> and another has an area of <b>200 m&sup2;</b>.<br>How much longer is the side of the larger field than the side of the smaller one?","opts":["10&radic;2 m","&radic;1600 m","20&radic;2 m","1600 m"],"a":"20&radic;2 m","hint":"Find each side length as a simplified surd before you subtract.","exp":"&radic;1800 = 30&radic;2 and &radic;200 = 10&radic;2, so the difference is <b>20&radic;2 m</b>. Subtracting the areas first and then taking one root gives &radic;1600, which is not the difference of the sides, and 1600 m is an area treated as a length."},{"id":"g8m-hd-004","q":"On a number line, point A is at <b>&minus;&radic;9</b> and point B is at <b>&radic;49</b>.<br>Point C is exactly midway between A and B. Where is C?","opts":["5 units, to the right of 0","2 units, to the left of 0","1.5 units, to the right of 0","2 units, to the right of 0"],"a":"2 units, to the right of 0","hint":"Work out the two roots first, then find the number halfway between them.","exp":"A is at &minus;3 and B is at 7, so C is at (&minus;3 + 7) &divide; 2 = 2, which is <b>2 units to the right of 0</b>. The answer at 5 units comes from halving 3 and 7 as if both were positive, and 1.5 units comes from halving 3 alone."}],"g8m-pythagoras":[{"id":"g8m-pyth-030","q":"What is the diagonal of a rectangle with length 8 cm and width 6 cm?","opts":["12 cm","7 cm","14 cm","10 cm"],"a":"10 cm","hint":"The diagonal creates a right-angled triangle with the length and width as legs.","exp":"d² = 8² + 6² = 64 + 36 = 100, so d = <b>10 cm</b>."},{"id":"g8m-pyth-061","q":"A pole is supported by a wire. The wire is 13 m long and is fastened to the ground 5 m from the base of the pole. How tall is the pole?","opts":["12 m","11 m","10 m","8 m"],"a":"12 m","hint":"pole² + 5² = 13².","exp":"pole² = 13² − 5² = 169 − 25 = 144, pole = √144 = <b>12 m</b>."},{"id":"g8m-pyth-008","q":"Which of the following is a valid Pythagorean triple (where all three values are positive integers)?","opts":["6, 8, 11","2, 3, 4","7, 24, 25","10, 12, 15"],"a":"7, 24, 25","hint":"Test each: a² + b² = c².","exp":"7² + 24² = 49 + 576 = 625 = 25². So <b>7, 24, 25</b> is a Pythagorean triple."},{"id":"g8m-hd-010","q":"A <b>17 m</b> ladder leans against a wall and reaches <b>15 m</b> up it.<br>The foot is then pulled out so the same ladder reaches only <b>8 m</b> up. How much further from the wall is the foot now?","opts":["8 m","15 m","23 m","7 m"],"a":"7 m","hint":"Work out the distance from the wall in both positions before comparing.","exp":"First position: &radic;(17&sup2; &minus; 15&sup2;) = &radic;64 = 8 m from the wall. Second position: &radic;(17&sup2; &minus; 8&sup2;) = &radic;225 = 15 m. The foot moves 15 &minus; 8 = <b>7 m</b>. Answering 15 m gives the new distance rather than the change, and 23 m comes from adding 15 and 8."},{"id":"g8m-hd-015","q":"A yacht sails <b>15 km</b> north from Grand Baie, then <b>20 km</b> east, then sails straight back to its starting point.<br>What total distance does it sail?","opts":["70 km","60 km","25 km","35 km"],"a":"60 km","hint":"The straight return is the hypotenuse of the two legs already sailed.","exp":"The return leg is &radic;(15&sup2; + 20&sup2;) = &radic;625 = 25 km, so the whole journey is 15 + 20 + 25 = <b>60 km</b>. 35 km counts only the first two legs, and 25 km counts only the return."}],"g8m-sequences":[{"id":"g8m-samp-001","q":"What is the next term in this sequence?<br><b>3, 7, 11, 15, ...</b>","opts":["19","20","17","23"],"a":"19","hint":"Find what is added each time.","exp":"Each term is 4 more than the one before: 3, 7, 11, 15. So the next term is 15 + 4 = 19. Adding 2 gives 17 and adding 8 gives 23, but neither matches the pattern."},{"id":"g8m-sequences-014","q":"A sequence goes <b>down by 5</b> each time. Its 4th term is 26. What is its <b>1st</b> term?","opts":["46","41","11","31"],"a":"41","hint":"Going backwards along the sequence means adding 5, not subtracting it.","exp":"From the 4th term back to the 1st is three steps, so add 5 three times: 26 + 15 = 41. 31 adds only one step, 46 adds four, and 11 subtracts instead of adding."},{"id":"g8m-sequences-010","q":"The nth term of a sequence is 5n − 3. Which term has value 47?","opts":["9th","10th","11th","8th"],"a":"10th","hint":"Set 5n − 3 = 47 and solve for n.","exp":"5n − 3 = 47 → 5n = 50 → n = 10. The 10th term equals 47."},{"id":"g8m-hd-017","q":"Chairs are set out in rows in a cyclone shelter. Row 1 has <b>5</b> chairs and every following row has <b>4 more</b> than the row before.<br>Which is the first row with more than <b>60</b> chairs, and how many chairs does it hold?","opts":["Row 15, with 60 chairs","Row 15, with 61 chairs","Row 14, with 57 chairs","Row 16, with 65 chairs"],"a":"Row 15, with 61 chairs","hint":"Build the rule 4n + 1 first, then find the smallest n that passes 60.","exp":"The nth row holds 4n + 1 chairs. Row 14 holds 57, which is not yet more than 60, and row 15 holds 4 &times; 15 + 1 = <b>61</b>. The choice of 60 chairs is not more than 60, and row 16 has already gone past the first one."},{"id":"g8m-hd-022","q":"In a sequence that goes up by the same amount each time, the <b>4th term is 23</b> and the <b>9th term is 48</b>.<br>Find the first term and the common difference.","opts":["First term 18, difference 5","First term 8, difference 5","First term 8, difference 25","First term 3, difference 5"],"a":"First term 8, difference 5","hint":"There are five steps between the 4th and the 9th term, not nine.","exp":"48 &minus; 23 = 25 over 5 steps, so the difference is 25 &divide; 5 = 5. Stepping back three times from the 4th term: 23 &minus; 15 = <b>8</b>. Taking 25 as the difference forgets to divide, and stepping back only once gives 18."}]},"grade8-science":{"g8s-inquiry":[{"id":"g8s-inquiry-002","q":"A scientist repeats an experiment three times and records each result. Why?","opts":["To impress the science teacher","Because the first result is wrong","Because one result cannot be published","To check the results are consistent"],"a":"To check the results are consistent","hint":"Repeated readings improve reliability.","exp":"Repeating an experiment lets the scientist <b>check that the results are consistent and calculate a reliable average</b>. This reduces the effect of random errors and makes the conclusion far more trustworthy."},{"id":"g8s-inquiry-014","q":"A ship is made of steel, which is denser than water. Why does it float?","opts":["Its engine constantly pushes it upwards","Its hollow shape lowers its average density","Seawater is dense enough to float anything","Steel is naturally a floating material"],"a":"Its hollow shape lowers its average density","hint":"A solid steel ball sinks; a hollow steel bowl floats.","exp":"A ship floats because its <b>average density — the steel hull plus all the air inside — is less than that of water</b>. The hollow hull displaces a large volume of water whose weight equals the ship's weight; this is Archimedes' principle."},{"id":"g8s-hd-006","q":"Why must a results table give the unit in the column heading rather than beside every value?","opts":["A table can only hold numbers in its cells","Units written repeatedly become inaccurate","It keeps the table readable and the unit unambiguous","It makes the values easier to add together"],"a":"It keeps the table readable and the unit unambiguous","hint":"The unit belongs to the quantity, not to each individual reading.","exp":"The heading states the quantity <b>and</b> its unit once, so every value below is unambiguous and the table stays readable. Repeating it in each cell adds clutter, not information."},{"id":"g8s-hd-001","q":"A block of metal has a mass of 270 g and measures 10 cm × 5 cm × 2 cm.<br>What is its density?","opts":["2.7 g/cm³","27 g/cm³","0.37 g/cm³","100 g/cm³"],"a":"2.7 g/cm³","hint":"Work out the volume first, then divide mass by volume.","exp":"Volume = 10 × 5 × 2 = 100 cm³. Density = 270 ÷ 100 = <b>2.7 g/cm³</b>, which is aluminium. Dividing by one side instead of the volume is the usual slip."},{"id":"g8s-hd-011","q":"A pupil repeats an experiment and gets almost identical values each time, but every value is wrong because the balance was never zeroed.<br>What does this show?","opts":["Precise results can still be inaccurate","Precision and accuracy mean the same thing","Repeating always removes every error","The balance must have been broken entirely"],"a":"Precise results can still be inaccurate","hint":"Two different words describe two different problems.","exp":"<b>Precision</b> is agreement between repeats; <b>accuracy</b> is closeness to the true value. A systematic error shifts every reading equally, so repeats agree beautifully and are all wrong."}],"g8s-food":[{"id":"g8s-food-005","q":"What is a balanced diet?","opts":["The right amount of every nutrient","The same amount of every food group","Only fruit and vegetables","A diet with no fat at all"],"a":"The right amount of every nutrient","hint":"All nutrients are needed — in the right proportions.","exp":"A balanced diet contains the <b>correct proportions</b> of carbohydrates, proteins, fats, vitamins, minerals, fibre and water. The amounts needed depend on age, activity level and health — equal amounts of every food would not be balanced."},{"id":"g8s-food-019","q":"A pupil eats bread with jam for breakfast, chips for lunch, and rice with oil for dinner. Which nutrient is most clearly missing from the whole day?","opts":["Fat","Starch","Protein","Sugar"],"a":"Protein","hint":"Look for the nutrient used to build and repair the body — which food on the list supplies it?","exp":"Every item listed is starch, fat or sugar, and nothing supplies protein for growth and repair — no fish, meat, egg, dal or milk appears. Starch comes from the bread, chips and rice, fat from the oil and chips, and sugar from the jam."},{"id":"g8s-food-011","q":"Which food test uses iodine solution to test for the presence of starch?","opts":["The Benedict test","The biuret test","The emulsion test","The iodine test"],"a":"The iodine test","hint":"Iodine is orange-brown but changes colour in the presence of starch.","exp":"In the <b>iodine test</b> a few drops of orange-brown iodine solution are added to the food; if starch is present the solution turns blue-black. Benedict's solution tests for sugars, the emulsion test for fats and biuret for proteins."},{"id":"g8s-hd-019","q":"Water is listed as an essential part of the diet although it supplies no energy.<br>Why?","opts":["Water is converted into energy when needed","Water replaces the need for eating fibre","Water supplies the minerals the body uses","Reactions in the body take place in water"],"a":"Reactions in the body take place in water","hint":"Being essential and being a fuel are different things.","exp":"Water is the <b>medium</b> for the body's chemistry, and the transport system for nutrients and waste. Essential and energy-providing are separate categories."},{"id":"g8s-hd-015","q":"A food is tested with iodine solution and it turns blue-black.<br>What does that show, and what does it NOT show?","opts":["Fat is present; it says nothing about sugar","Vitamin C is present; it rules out starch","Protein is present; it says nothing about starch","Starch is present; it says nothing about protein"],"a":"Starch is present; it says nothing about protein","hint":"A positive test identifies one nutrient only.","exp":"Iodine turning blue-black is the <b>starch</b> test. A food may also contain protein or fat, but this test cannot see them — one test answers one question."}],"g8s-mixtures":[{"id":"g8s-mixtures-004","q":"Which principle allows a magnet to separate iron filings from sand?","opts":["Iron dissolves but sand does not","Iron is larger than sand","Iron is heavier than sand","Iron is magnetic, sand is not"],"a":"Iron is magnetic, sand is not","hint":"Only magnetic materials are attracted to a magnet.","exp":"The magnet attracts the iron filings but not the sand, because <b>iron is a magnetic material and sand is not</b>. This is the principle behind magnetic separation."},{"id":"g8s-mixtures-010","q":"Why does filtration work for separating an insoluble solid from a liquid?","opts":["Solid particles react with the filter paper","Solid particles are heavier than the liquid","Solid particles are too big for the pores","The liquid evaporates as it passes through"],"a":"Solid particles are too big for the pores","hint":"Filtration is a physical sieving process based on particle size.","exp":"Filtration works because the insoluble solid's particles are <b>larger than the tiny pores in the filter paper</b>, so they stay behind as the residue, while the much smaller liquid molecules pass through as the filtrate."},{"id":"g8s-mixtures-020","q":"Ethanol and water are mixed. Filtration cannot separate them, but distillation can. Why?","opts":["They boil at different temperatures","They have the same density","They are both insoluble in water","They freeze at the same temperature"],"a":"They boil at different temperatures","hint":"Distillation separates by turning one substance into a gas before the other.","exp":"Ethanol boils at 78 °C and water at 100 °C, so on gentle heating the ethanol vapour comes off first and is condensed. They do not freeze at the same temperature, ethanol mixes with water completely rather than being insoluble, and their densities differ — but none of those is what distillation uses."},{"id":"g8s-hd-031","q":"A mixture contains iron filings, sand and salt.<br>What is the most efficient order of separation?","opts":["Filter first, then use a magnet, then dissolve","Dissolve everything first, then use a magnet","Magnet, then dissolve and filter, then evaporate","Evaporate first, then filter, then use a magnet"],"a":"Magnet, then dissolve and filter, then evaporate","hint":"Remove the component that needs no water first.","exp":"Take the iron out <b>dry</b> with a magnet first — once wet it is far harder. Then dissolve the salt, filter off the sand, and evaporate to recover the salt. Order matters."},{"id":"g8s-hd-027","q":"A mixture of ethanol (boiling point 78 °C) and water is heated to 80 °C.<br>What is collected first?","opts":["Neither, because 80 °C is too low","Water, because there is more of it present","Both together, because they are mixed","Ethanol, because it boils at a lower temperature"],"a":"Ethanol, because it boils at a lower temperature","hint":"Fractional distillation separates by boiling point.","exp":"The component with the <b>lower boiling point</b> vaporises first, so ethanol comes over before water. Quantity does not decide the order; boiling point does."}]},"grade8-social-modern-studies":{"g8sms-slavery":[{"id":"g8sms-slavery-014","q":"The Morne Brabant peninsula is a UNESCO World Heritage Site because:","opts":["It sheltered runaway slaves","It is the highest mountain","It is where the French landed","It was the indenture depot"],"a":"It sheltered runaway slaves","hint":"Le Morne is a symbol of resistance against slavery.","exp":"<b>Le Morne Brabant</b> was a refuge for escaped enslaved people (maroons) who hid in its forests. According to tradition, when soldiers climbed to inform them of emancipation, the maroons, fearing re-enslavement, threw themselves off the cliff. It is a powerful symbol of the <b>struggle for freedom</b> and is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."},{"id":"g8sms-slavery-002","q":"What were the main conditions of enslaved people on sugar plantations in Mauritius?","opts":["No rights and no pay","The right to own land","The same rights as freemen","Wages and weekly rest"],"a":"No rights and no pay","hint":"Slavery is the ownership of people as property — they had no freedom.","exp":"Enslaved people in Mauritius had <b>no legal rights</b>. They were treated as property of their owners, were forced to work without pay, lived in poor conditions (labour camps) and faced harsh punishment for disobedience."},{"id":"g8sms-slavery-003","q":"Slavery in Mauritius was used primarily to:","opts":["Provide labour for sugar","Settle the island quickly","Serve in houses only","Build the public roads only"],"a":"Provide labour for sugar","hint":"The plantation economy depended entirely on enslaved labour.","exp":"Slavery in Mauritius was an <b>economic labour system</b>. Planters needed a large workforce to grow and harvest sugarcane. Enslaved Africans provided this <b>forced, unpaid labour</b>, making sugar production highly profitable for the plantation owners."},{"id":"g8sms-slavery-020","q":"Why is the indenture period (1834–1920) described as transformative for Mauritian society?","opts":["It left no lasting impact","It reshaped the population","It reduced our diversity","It touched the economy only"],"a":"It reshaped the population","hint":"Demographic change has long-lasting social and political consequences.","exp":"The indenture period was <b>demographically transformative</b>: it made people of Indian origin the largest community in Mauritius. Indian languages, religions (Hinduism, Islam), foods, music and cultural practices became woven into Mauritian society — permanently shaping its culture and politics."},{"id":"g8sms-slavery-004","q":"The \"Code Noir\" during the French period was:","opts":["A French newspaper","A type of Mauritian sugar","A law code on slavery","A French army handbook"],"a":"A law code on slavery","hint":"It was a legal code relating to the institution of slavery.","exp":"The <b>Code Noir</b> (Black Code) was a set of French colonial laws that defined the conditions of slavery, including the \"rights\" and duties of slave owners and the rules governing enslaved people. Despite its claims to regulate treatment, it fundamentally upheld the system of enslavement."}],"g8sms-society":[{"id":"g8sms-society-005","q":"Trade unions became important in Mauritius in the 1930s–1960s because:","opts":["They stopped all strikes","They had no political role","They were set up by the planters","They pressed for better wages"],"a":"They pressed for better wages","hint":"Trade unions represent workers in negotiations with employers.","exp":"<b>Trade unions</b> played a crucial role in the 1930s–1960s by organising workers to demand <b>better wages, shorter working hours and improved conditions</b>. Leaders like Emmanuel Anquetil were important figures in the early labour movement."},{"id":"g8sms-society-014","q":"The Export Processing Zone (EPZ), established in the 1970s, transformed Mauritius by:","opts":["Expanding sugarcane growing","Developing finance only","Building luxury tourist hotels","Creating many factory jobs"],"a":"Creating many factory jobs","hint":"The EPZ brought manufacturing industry to a primarily agricultural economy.","exp":"The <b>Export Processing Zone (EPZ)</b>, established in the early 1970s, encouraged foreign investment in <b>textile and garment factories</b>. It created tens of thousands of jobs (especially for women), reduced unemployment and was the first step in Mauritius's economic diversification away from sugar."},{"id":"g8sms-society-027","q":"From the 1970s, Mauritius developed two major new industries alongside sugar. Which pair is correct?","opts":["Oil refining and steel production","Fishing and rubber growing","Gold mining and coffee growing","Tourism and textile manufacturing"],"a":"Tourism and textile manufacturing","hint":"One industry filled the new Export Processing Zone factories; the other used the island's beautiful beaches.","exp":"The Export Processing Zone (EPZ), established in 1970, attracted textile factories; at the same time the government promoted Mauritius as a tourist destination. Both industries created tens of thousands of jobs and helped free the economy from its dangerous dependence on a single crop. Mauritius has no oil, no significant gold deposits, and rubber is a tropical crop suited to different conditions."},{"id":"g8sms-society-020","q":"The Export Processing Zone opened many textile factories from the 1970s. Which group entered paid work in the largest numbers because of it?","opts":["Sailors","Fishermen","Planters","Women"],"a":"Women","hint":"Ask which group had mostly worked unpaid at home before the factories opened.","exp":"Textile factories recruited women in very large numbers, and paid work outside the home changed family incomes and women’s place in Mauritian society. Sailors, planters and fishermen were already in paid work and were not what the new factories were hiring for."},{"id":"g8sms-society-003","q":"The cyclone of 1945 and the post-war period accelerated social change in Mauritius because:","opts":["Britain freed Mauritius in 1945","Everything improved at once","Sugar was destroyed for ever","Hardship raised political demands"],"a":"Hardship raised political demands","hint":"Crisis can motivate people to demand change.","exp":"Post-war hardships and the 1945 cyclone intensified <b>political awareness</b> among Mauritians. Workers and political leaders demanded better wages, conditions and greater <b>democratic representation</b>, accelerating the movement towards independence."}],"g8sms-independence":[{"id":"g8sms-independence-010","q":"The French Empire in Africa was significant for Mauritius because:","opts":["France ruled here 1810-1968","France ruled here 1715-1810","France granted freedom freely","France and Mauritius never met"],"a":"France ruled here 1715-1810","hint":"The French period left a lasting cultural legacy in Mauritius.","exp":"France controlled Mauritius as <b>\"Île de France\"</b> from <b>1715 to 1810</b>. The French period introduced sugarcane cultivation, brought enslaved Africans, and left a lasting legacy: the French language, legal traditions and much of the cultural character of Mauritius."},{"id":"g8sms-independence-003","q":"European colonial powers justified colonialism by claiming it brought \"civilisation\" to colonised peoples. Why is this justification rejected today?","opts":["Europe had no impact at all","All colonised peoples agreed","It exploited and denied rights","The colonies were developed"],"a":"It exploited and denied rights","hint":"The reality of colonialism involved exploitation, not just \"development\".","exp":"The \"civilising mission\" was a <b>self-serving justification</b> for colonialism that ignored colonial realities: exploitation of resources and labour, suppression of local cultures and languages, racism and the denial of self-determination. Modern scholarship rejects this justification entirely."},{"id":"g8sms-independence-005","q":"Which major global event accelerated decolonisation after 1945?","opts":["The rise of the internet","The French Revolution","The Second World War","The Industrial Revolution"],"a":"The Second World War","hint":"WWII left European powers weakened both economically and morally.","exp":"<b>World War II (1939–1945)</b> was a key turning point for decolonisation. European powers were economically and militarily weakened. Colonial soldiers who had fought for European nations returned demanding rights. The <b>United Nations</b>, founded in 1945, promoted self-determination."},{"id":"g8sms-independence-015","q":"The Chagos Archipelago dispute between Mauritius and the United Kingdom concerns:","opts":["A border row about Rodrigues","A quarrel over the sugar trade","Fishing rights near Agaléga","The Chagos Islands were taken"],"a":"The Chagos Islands were taken","hint":"The ICJ and UN General Assembly have ruled in Mauritius's favour.","exp":"The <b>Chagos Archipelago</b> was separated from Mauritius in 1965 before independence. The UK created the British Indian Ocean Territory (BIOT) and leased Diego Garcia to the USA for a military base. The <b>International Court of Justice (2019)</b> found that the separation was unlawful and called for the return of the islands to Mauritius."},{"id":"g8sms-independence-012","q":"The Lancaster House Constitutional Conferences in London in 1965 and 1967 were significant for Mauritius because:","opts":["They settled independence terms","They ended slavery in Mauritius","They created the sugar industry","They opened the first schools"],"a":"They settled independence terms","hint":"Lancaster House was where British colonies negotiated their independence constitutions.","exp":"The <b>Lancaster House conferences</b> negotiated Mauritius' independence constitution. Controversially, Mauritius was required to cede the <b>Chagos Archipelago (Diego Garcia)</b> to Britain as a condition of independence — a territorial dispute that continues today."}]}}};
